<h2>Calibrated Plywood vs Blockboard</h2>
<h3>Structural Differences</h3>
<p>Blockboard has a core of solid wood strips sandwiched between face veneers, making it significantly stiffer along one axis — ideal for long shelves and table tops that must resist sagging. Plywood has a more isotropic (equal strength in all directions) structure. Blockboard is generally not used in CNC-based production because its core construction makes it unsuitable for edge routing and precise joinery.</p>
<h3>Best Use of Each</h3>
<p>Blockboard remains the preferred material for long spans — a 1200 mm or wider shelf, a dining table top, a bed headboard — where sagging is a concern. Calibrated plywood is the correct choice for carcass panels, drawer bottoms, and any component that requires precise hardware fitting.</p>
<h2>Calibrated Plywood vs Commercial Plywood</h2>
<h3>Manufacturing and Quality Differences</h3>
<p>Commercial plywood (IS:303 compliant, MR grade) is the most basic and affordable category — suitable for interior use in dry conditions. It uses lower-grade veneers, lower formaldehyde-rated adhesives, and has no calibration. It is perfectly serviceable for painting-grade furniture, interior partitions, and budget applications where precision fit is not required.</p>
<h3>Cost and Lifespan</h3>
<p>Commercial plywood costs 30–50% less than equivalent-thickness calibrated plywood from a premium brand. In a dry interior environment with good ventilation, commercial plywood can last 10–15 years without structural failure. The visual and functional quality of furniture made from it, however, begins to show its limitations within the first few years as wood movement and initial thickness variance become apparent.</p>
<h2>Is Calibrated Plywood Stronger Than Normal Plywood?</h2>
<div class="callout"><span class="callout__title">Straight Answer</span><strong>No — calibration alone does not increase strength.</strong> Strength is determined by wood species, veneer thickness, number of plies, adhesive quality, and manufacturing process. A calibrated 18 mm board and a non-calibrated 18 mm board from the same grade and brand will have the same load-bearing and flexural strength.</div>
<h3>What Calibration Actually Improves</h3>
<p>Calibration improves <em>dimensional predictability</em>, not structural performance. The confusion arises because premium brands offering calibrated plywood also tend to use better veneers, tighter glue lines, and more controlled production — so the finished product often is stronger. But that's a brand-quality effect, not a calibration effect.</p>
<h3>Load-Bearing Capacity: What Matters</h3>
<p>For shelves supporting heavy loads — books, crockery, stone countertops — focus on plywood grade, core veneer quality, and span. A well-specified BWP-grade 19 mm non-calibrated board will outperform a poorly specified calibrated 18 mm board in load-bearing terms.</p>
